{"indexedChars":10800,"totalChars":10800,"version":"581","text":"Skip to content\n1\n/\n15\nsearch\ngroup_add\nmore_horiz\nget_app\nReceived: 11 August 2022\n \nRevised: 2 December 2022\n \nAccepted: 8 January 2023\n \nIET Generation, Transmission & Distribution\n\n\nDOI: 10.1049/gtd2.12763\n\n\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\n\n\nA deep LSTM-CNN based on self-attention mechanism with input\n\n\ndata reduction for short-term load forecasting\n\n\nShiyan Yi\n \n1\n \nHaichun Liu\n \n2\n \nTao Chen\n3,4\n \nJianwen Zhang\n5\n \nYibo Fan\n1\n\n\n1\n \nThe State Key Laboratory of ASIC and System, Fudan University, Shanghai, China\n\n\n2\n \nDepartment of Automation, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China\n\n\n3\n \nDepartment of Economics, Department of Statistic and Actuarial Science, Big Data Research Lab, University of Waterloo, Waterloo, Canada\n\n\n4\n \nSenior Research Fellow, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA\n\n\n5\n \nThe Key Laboratory of Control of Power Transmission and Conversion of Ministry of Education, Department of Electrical Engineering, School of Electronic Information and Electrical\n\n\nEngineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Minhang District, Shanghai, China\n\n\nCorrespondence\n\n\nJianwen Zhang, The Key Laboratory of Control of\n\n\nPower Transmission and Conversion of Ministry of\n\n\nEducation, Department of Electrical Engineering,\n\n\nSchool of Electronic Information and Electrical\n\n\nEngineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University,\n\n\nMinhang District, Shanghai 200240, China.\n\n\nEmail: icebergzjw@sjtu.edu.cn\n\n\nYibo Fan, The State Key Laboratory of ASIC and\n\n\nSystem, Fudan University, Shanghai 201203, China.\n\n\nEmail: fanyibo@fudan.edu.cn\n\n\nFunding information\n\n\nFudan-ZTE Joint Lab; National Natural Science\n\n\nFoundation of China, Grant/Award Numbers:\n\n\n52277193, 62031009; Pioneering Project of\n\n\nAcademy for Engineering and Technology Fudan\n\n\nUniversity, Grant/Award Number: gyy2021-001;\n\n\nFudan University-CIOMP Joint Fund, Grant/Award\n\n\nNumber: FC2019-001; CCF-Alibaba Innovative\n\n\nResearch Fund For Young Scholars; Alibaba\n\n\nInnovative Research (AIR) Program; Shanghai\n\n\nCommittee of Science and Technology,\n\n\nGrant/Award Number: 19DZ1205403\n\n\nAbstract\n\n\nNumerous studies on short-term load forecasting (STLF) have used feature extraction\n\n\nmethods to increase the model’s accuracy by incorporating multidimensional features con-\n\n\ntaining time, weather and distance information. However, less attention has been paid to\n\n\nthe input data size and output dimensions in STLF. To address these two issues, an STLF\n\n\nmodel is proposed based on output dimensions using only load data. First, the load data’s\n\n\nlong-term behavior (trend and seasonality) is extracted through the long short-term mem-\n\n\nory network (LSTM), followed by convolution to obtain the load data’s non-stationarity.\n\n\nThen, using the self-attention mechanism (SAM), the crucial input load information is\n\n\nemphasized in the forecasting process. The calculation example shows that the proposed\n\n\nalgorithm outperforms LSTM, LSTM-based SAM, and CNN-GRU-based SAM by more\n\n\nthan 10% in eight different buildings, demonstrating its suitability for forecasting with only\n\n\nload data. Additionally, compared to earlier research utilizing two well-known public data\n\n\nsets, the MAPE is optimized by 2.2% and 5%, respectively. Also, the method has good\n\n\nprediction accuracy for a wide variety of time granularities and load aggregation levels, so\n\n\nit can be applied to various load forecasting scenarios and has good reference signiﬁcance\n\n\nfor load forecasting instrumentation.\n\n\n1\n \nINTRODUCTION\n\n\nShort-term load forecasting (STLF) is a technique that fore-\n\n\ncasts the next few hours’ electric loads based on the changing\n\n\npattern of load data. STLF is the foundation for micro-\n\n\ngrid “source–grid–load–storage” operation and dispatch [1, 2].\n\n\nSTLF can coordinate ﬂexible energy storage (ES) systems or\n\n\nadvanced demand response (DR) technologies for peak regula-\n\n\ntion [3]. In addition, STLF can effectively reduce the uncertainty\n\n\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the\n\n\noriginal work is properly cited, the use is non-commercial and no modiﬁcations or adaptations are made.\n\n\n© 2023 The Authors.\n \nIET Generation, Transmission & Distribution\n \npublished by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of The Institution of Engineering and Technology.\n\n\nof distributed generation resources (DGRs) such as small\n\n\nand medium photovoltaic (PV) generators and ﬂexible loads\n\n\nsuch as renewable electric vehicles (EVs) in order to achieve\n\n\namicable microgrid and grid access [4, 5]. For reliable load\n\n\nforecasting, the majority of existing load forecasting algorithms\n\n\nrequire multidimensional data inputs such as the load, mete-\n\n\norological, and calendar [6–9]. However, precise ﬁne-grained\n\n\nmeteorological data and long-term calendar data are challeng-\n\n\ning to obtain in microgrid applications [10, 11]. The short-term\n\n\n1538\n \nwileyonlinelibrary.com/iet-gtd\n \nIET Gener. Transm. Distrib.\n \n2023;17:1538–1552.\nYI\n \nET AL\n \n.\n \n1539\n\n\nTABLE 1\n \nRelated works on short-term load forecasting\n\n\nCategory\n \nAuthor\n \nMethod\n \nDescription\n \nAdvantage\n \nShortcoming\n\n\nDNN\n \nX. Kong [21]\n \nDBN\n \nImproved DBN for STLF\n \nCan abstract nonlinear interactions\n\n\nlayer by layer\n\n\nCannot extract temporal\n\n\nfeatures\n\n\nK. Chen [22]\n \nResNet\n \nAn end-to-end model\n\n\nW. Kong [23]\n \nLSTM\n \nExtract temporal features\n \nMore accurate than other DNNs\n \nThe structure is too simple.\n\n\nFeature\n\n\nextraction\n\n\nKim [6]\n \nCNN-LSTM\n \nAccurate but many parameters\n \nHandle raw data at a higher degree\n\n\nof abstraction\n\n\nNeeds many input features as\n\n\ntemperature, humidity, date\n\n\ninformation. Besides, the SD\n\n\ninformation may not be\n\n\nsuitable for STLF.\n\n\nYao [24]\n \nCNN-GRU\n \nGRU can reduce parameters.\n\n\nH. Chen [25]\n \nSD-FOA\n \nEach factor’s weight assigned\n\n\nby optimization algorithm\nZheng [26]\n \nSD-Xgboost\n\n\nPark [27]\n \nSD-RL\n \nUsing RL to choose SD\n\n\nLoad modeling\n \nW. Kong [28]\n \nLSTM\n \nCan learn residential behavior\n \nAppliance-level forecasting\n\n\ntechniques provide ﬁner\n\n\ngranularity and more accuracy\n\n\nNeeds appliances’ load\n\n\nJi [29]\n \nHSMM\n \nCan adjust different situation\n \nNecessitates complex dynamical\n\n\nmodels\n\n\nData\n\n\ndecompose\n\n\nLiu and\n\n\nZhao [30]\n\n\nK-means\n\n\nEMD\n\n\nUsing K-means to classify\n\n\nIMFs and Res\n\n\nCan handle low-dimensional data\n \nRequires massive amount of\n\n\ncalculations and time\n\n\nDeng [31]\n \nEEMD\n \nReduce mode mixing in EMD\n\n\nModel selection\n \nFeng [32]\n \nQ-learning\n \nSelects the most appropriate\n\n\nmodel in the model pool\n\n\nAble to handle complex situations\n \nModel pool affects accuracy,\n\n\ncalculations lead to\n\n\ntremendous complexity\n\n\nLi [33]\n \nMeta-learning\n\n\nload data generated by distributed smart meters can represent\n\n\nmeteorological and calendar information. Effective mining of\n\n\nreal-time load data can produce accurate load forecasts with-\n\n\nout the need for meteorological and calendar data. This can\n\n\neffectively reduce the required data dimension and avoid the\n\n\ntransmission and storage of large amounts of data, meeting the\n\n\nrequirements of the independent operation and autonomous\n\n\narchitecture of the microgrid, the improved overall performance\n\n\nof the microgrid, and the friendliness of the system dispatch.\n\n\nConventional STLF approaches often relate to statistical\n\n\nmodels, such as the autoregressive moving average (ARMA)\n\n\n[12], autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA) [13],\n\n\nand exponential smoothing model [14]. These approaches are\n\n\nstraightforward to implement and interpretable, but their ability\n\n\nto anticipate nonlinear series is low, so they cannot effec-\n\n\ntively handle the STLF problem. In comparison to conventional\n\n\nSTLF approaches, machine learning has better nonlinear ﬁt-\n\n\nting capabilities. The back-propagation neural network [15] and\n\n\nsupport vector regression (SVR) [16] are commonly employed\n\n\nmachine learning time-series prediction algorithms. The back-\n\n\npropagation neural network has excellent parameter ﬁtting\n\n\nskills, but owing to its simple structure, it has low generaliza-\n\n\ntion abilities and is prone to the local optimum. As SVR is\n\n\nbased on the learning criterion of structural reduction, it has a\n\n\nhigh degree of generalizability. SVR performs well with small\n\n\ndata sample sizes, but it fails to converge with high sample\n\n\nsizes. Machine learning can learn nonlinear correlations between\n\n\nsequences better than conventional methods, but it typically\n\n\nhas drawbacks including weak generalization capabilities, trou-\n\n\nble establishing hyper-parameters, and prediction accuracy that\n\n\nfrequently depends on the quality and quantity of input data.\n\n\nDeep learning superimposes multilayer neural networks, which\n\n\nmay realize multilayer nonlinear mapping. With their nonlin-\n\n\near mapping and adaptable capacities, systems based on deep\n\n\nlearning provide a viable solution for load forecasting [17–20].\n\n\nTable 1 shows the related works on STLF. X. Kong [21] pro-\n\n\nposed an improved deep belief network (DBN) method from\n\n\nthe three aspects of model structure, parameter optimization,\n\n\nand data selection for better use in load forecasting. In [22], a\n\n\nprediction method of a novel end-to-end neural network model\n\n\nusing deep residual neural networks (ResNets) was proposed.\n\n\nDeep learning can abstract nonlinear interactions layer by\n\n\nlayer, which can increase the accuracy of STLF in comparison to\n\n\nmachine learning. However, a deep neural network (DNN), like\n\n\nDBN and ResNet, is insensitive to temporal correlation, mak-\n\n\ning it difﬁcult to obtain better prediction outcomes. Considering\n\n\nenergy consumption is a typical time series, the model’s output\n\n\nshould relate to the past input. LSTM, a variant of the recurrent\n\n\nneural network (RNN), is highly competitive in various ﬁelds\n\n\nand can take the temporal dependencies of the time series. In\n\n\n[23], LSTM was employed to predict loads for individual users,\n\n\nand its superiority to traditional methods was demonstrated. In\n\n\nconsideration of the temporal features of the load data, LSTM\n\n\nwas used as a basis for the model in consideration of the tem-\n\n\nporal characteristics of the load data.There are numerous STLF\n\n\nmethods based on LSTM. The most common method is fea-\n\n\nture extraction. CNN is the most popular feature extraction\n\n\nmethod in STLF, which can extract high-dimensional correla-\n\n\ntions of a feature set [34–36]. Kim [6] proposed a CNN-LSTM\n\n\nneural network, which can improve prediction accuracy by ana-\n\n\nlyzing features that inﬂuence the load. In [24], LSTM is being\n\n\nreplaced by a gated recurrent unit (GRU) to reduce the number\n\n\nof parameters. Similar-day selection is another feature extraction\n\n\nmethod in STLF used to discover the similar forecasting day\n×\n拖拽到此处完成下载\n图片将完成下载\nAIX智能下载器"}